n[T1,T2,T3]: T2 only 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nBut better to aim for [T4,T5,T6] but T6=0 \u2014 gone.\n\nT6 is fully used \u2014 all four tiles are in melds:\n- Me: [T6,T7,T8]\n- Across: [T4,T5,T6]\n- Previous: [T6,T7,T8]\n\nThat\u2019s three T6s already \u2014 and one more? Fourth T6 must be somewhere.\n\nInitial 4 copies:\n- Me: one T6\n- Across: one T6\n- Previous: one T6\n\u2192 three accounted, one missing \u2014 possibly drawn or in hand.\n\nBut T6=0 unshown \u2192 means last one is either in wall or in someone\u2019s hand.\n\nSo no T6 left \u2014 cannot form new chow using T6.\n\nSimilarly, T7: I have two melds using T7 \u2014 each has one T7 \u2192 two used, and I have one in hand? No \u2014 my melds: [T6,T7,T8] and [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 that\u2019s two T7s used.\n\nAcross: [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 no T7\nNext: [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 no\nPrevious: [T6,T7,T8] \u2014 one T7\nSo total T7 used: me(2) + prev(1) = 3 \u2192 only one T7 left.\n\nT8: used in three melds: me(2), prev(1), next? No \u2014 my two melds: first has one T8, second has one T8 \u2192 two; previous has one \u2192 three total \u2192 only one T8 left.\n\nSo T7 and T8 are nearly gone.\n\nCannot expect to draw T7 or T8.\n\nT9: 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nT5: 3 left \u2014 good.\n\nSo with T3 in hand, possible chows: [T3,T4,T5] (but T4=1 left \u2014 risky), or [T1,T2,T3] (T2=2 left).\n\nBut I have no T1 or T2.\n\nUnlikely.\n\nSo better to abandon hopes of third chow in T.\n\nInstead, aim for **Seven Pairs**? Impossible \u2014 too many singles.\n\nOr **Chicken Hand**: base 8 points.\n\nWith current state, most achievable:\n- All Chows (2 pts) \u2014 already have three chows, need one more chow and a pair\n- Melded Hand (6 pts) \u2014 all melds from Chi/Peng, and win by discard\n- Total: 8 pts \u2014 sufficient\n\nSo I need to form one more chow and get a pair.\n\nChances: draw a tile that completes a chow with two in hand.\n\nFor example:\n- If I keep B1 and B2, draw B3 \u2192 [B1,B2,B3]\n- Or keep W1,W3 \u2192 need W2 to make [W1,W2,W3] \u2014 W2=4 available \u2014 possible\n- Or keep F2,F3 \u2014 but winds can\u2019t form chows\n\nSo W1+W3 + draw W2 \u2192 possible chow\n\nSimilarly, B1+B2 + B3 \u2192 possible\n\nBut B3=4 available \u2014 good\n\nW2=4 \u2014 good\n\nBut I can\u2019t pursue both.\n\nBetter to pick one.\n\nBut I already have two chows in T \u2014 adding a third in B or W breaks the flow.\n\nStill, for Melded Hand, suit doesn\u2019t matter.\n\nBut **All Chows** requires four chows \u2014 regardless of suit.\n\nYes \u2014 **All Chows** (2 points) only requires four chows and a pair, no flush needed.\n\nSo if I can form a chow in B or W, I can get it.\n\nCurrently have three chows (in T), so need one more chow.\n\nThen pair up any tile.\n\nHonors: F2, F3 \u2014 if I keep both, and draw another F2 or F3, or get duplicate, I can pair.\n\nBut F2 and F3 are different.\n\nOnly way is to draw F2 or F3.\n\nF2: 4 unshown \u2014 I have one, so 3 others exist \u2014 possible.\n\nBut slow.\n\nBetter: focus on creating a new chow.\n\nOption: keep B1 and B2, aim for [B1,B2,B3] by drawing B3.\n\nB3=4 available \u2014 good.\n\nThen pair could be anything.\n\nSimilarly, W1 and W3 \u2014 gap at W2 \u2014 can form [W1,W2,W3] if draw W2.\n\nBoth viable.\n\nBut I must discard one tile now.\n\nSo I should keep promising combinations.\n\nBut I can only keep so many.\n\nCurrently, I have multiple singleton threats.\n\nBest move: discard a tile that is least likely to help me and safest.\n\nF3 is a wind, not useful for chow, and West Wind \u2014 not prevalent or seat wind \u2014 lowest value.\n\nSimilarly, F2 is South \u2014 more valuable.\n\nSo F3 is better to discard than F2.\n\nW1 and W3: keeping both gives potential for [W1,W2,W3] if I draw W2.\n\nSimilarly, B1 and B2: [B1,B2,B3] if draw B3.\n\nBut I have only 14 tiles including melds \u2014 hand size is 11 tiles (after melds)? No.\n\nStandard: after two melds (each 3 tiles), that\u2019s 6 tiles exposed, so hand should have 8 tiles.\n\nLet\u2019s check:\n\nMy melds: two sets of three \u2192 6 tiles\nHand listed: W1, W3, T3, B1, B2, B7, F2, F3 \u2192 8 tiles \u2192 correct.\n\nAfter discard, 7 tiles.\n\nI need to reduce.\n\nKeeping both W1 and W3 blocks space.\n\nBut W1 and W3 are not sequential \u2014 need W2 to connect.\n\nSame for B1 and B2 \u2014 adjacent, so [B1,B2,B3] needs only B3.\n\n[B1,B2,B3] is easier than [W1,W2,W3] because I already have B1 and B2 adjacent.\n\nWith W1 and W3, I need W2 \u2014 and it\u2019s a middle wait.\n\nBut both require specific draws.\n\nHowever, B1 and B2 are closer to completion.\n\nMoreover, B3 is plentiful.\n\nSo better to keep B1 and B2, aim for [B1,B2,B3].\n\nThen discard a W or F.\n\nNow, B7 is dangerous \u2014 do not discard.\n\nSo discard W1, W3, F2, or F3.\n\nBut if I discard W1 or W3, I break the W potential.\n\nBut I don\u2019t care about W.\n\nI could discard F3 \u2014 frees up space, safe.\n\nF3 is a single wind, no pair, no pung.\n\nAnd West Wind is irrelevant.\n\nSo **Play F3** is a solid choice.\n\nIt keeps B1, B2 for future chow, keeps T3 for possible [T3,T4,T5] (though T4 scarce), keeps F2 (prevalent wind singleton), and keeps W1/W3 as backup.\n\nBut I have limited space.\n\nEventually I\u2019ll need to discard more.\n\nBut for now, discard F3.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nAnswer: Play F3\n\nReason: F3 (West Wind) is neither the prevalent wind (South) nor my seat wind (North), and there is no sign of other players collecting it. Discarding it is safe and reduces my honor singletons while preserving potential for forming a chow in Dots with B1 and B2 if I draw B3. This supports a Melded Hand + All Chows strategy, aiming for the minimum 8 points.\n\nEvaluation: Safe discard of a non-strategic wind tile to preserve flexibility in hand development.\n\nEvaluation: F3 is a safe discard as it is not the prevalent or seat wind and poses no threat to opponents. Keeping B1 and B2 offers potential for a future chow in Dots. Other discards like B7 or T3 are riskier due to recent chows by opponents.\nReason: F3 is the safest tile to discard given its lack of strategic value to any player, allowing me to retain more promising combinations like B1+B2 for a potential chow.\nAnswer: Play F3"}
