ft in the wall** (all zero copies unshown). That means:\n    - The only way to get more bamboo tiles is through discards.\n    - My two chows are complete, but I cannot extend them further.\n    - Any hope for additional T-based chows is gone.\n\nThus, I must shift focus to minimizing loss or aiming for minimal-scoring viable hand.\n\nPotential patterns:\n- **Chicken Hand (8 points)**: If nothing else scores, this default pattern applies when total fan \u22658. But I need at least 8 points.\n- Currently possible scoring elements:\n    - *One Voided Suit*: I have W (Characters), T (Bamboos), B (Dots), F (Winds) \u2192 three suits + honors \u2192 not applicable.\n    - *No Honors*: I have F2 and F4 \u2192 invalid.\n    - *All Chows*: I already have two chows; if I turn remaining into chows... but hard with honor tiles.\n    - *Mixed Double Chow*: Possible if I form another chow in same numbers? Not feasible.\n    - *Edge Wait / Closed Wait / Single Wait*: Possible depending on final wait, but too early.\n    - *Self Drawn*: Would add 1 point if I draw winning tile.\n\nBut none of the high-value patterns are reachable.\n\nBest strategy: **Discard safest tile that doesn\u2019t feed opponents and prepares for a minimal valid hand.**\n\nAmong options:\n- **F4 (North Wind)**: Seat wind. Could be useful for 2-point \"Seat Wind\" if I win with a pair or pung. But only one, and J1/J2/J3 all gone \u2014 dragons are dead. Winds: F2 (South) prevalent (3 copies left), F4 (North) seat wind. Others may want F4.\n- **F2 (South Wind)**: Prevalent wind! Worth 2 points if anyone uses it. Opponents may be collecting it. Previous player discarded F1/F3/F4, not holding many winds. Still, F2 is valuable \u2014 better kept than F4?\n- **T3**: Dangerous. Next player did **Chi T3**, meaning they completed a chow using T3 \u2014 very likely they have T1/T2 or T2/T4. So T3 helps them complete sequences. High risk.\n- **B1/B2**: Dots \u2014 B5-B6-B7 was melded by next player. B6/B7 gone. B5 also gone. So B3/B4 needed for chows \u2014 both low supply. B1/B2 unlikely helpful to others. Safer.\n- **W1/W3/W6**: Characters \u2014 no one has shown interest. Only one W8 left. W4/W5/W6 used by opponent. But W5/W6 are mid-game. Still, little threat.\n\nFinal consideration: I must reduce hand size toward 14-tile winning structure. I currently have 8 hand tiles and 2 melds (6 tiles) = 14 tiles. Wait \u2014 **in mahjong, after forming a meld, you draw a tile back? No \u2014 when you perform a Chi/Peng/Gang, you add the meld to your side, remove 2/3 tiles from hand, and do NOT draw**. So total tiles in hand fluctuate.\n\nWait: Standard rule: After making an exposed meld (via Chi/Peng/Gang), you place the 3/4 tiles face-up, and then it becomes your turn to discard one tile. Your hand decreases by 2 tiles (for Chi: you contributed 2 from hand + 1 claimed; for Peng: 2 from hand + 1 claimed).\n\nLet\u2019s reconstruct:\n\nInitial hand: 13 tiles  \nThen: Chi T7 \u2192 added T7 to form [T6,T7,T8] \u2192 used two tiles from hand (say T6,T8?) \u2192 hand now: 13 - 2 = 11, plus new meld  \nThen later: Chi T8 \u2192 formed [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 again used two tiles from hand \u2192 hand: 11 - 2 = 9  \nPlus draws during game \u2014 but each normal turn: draw one, discard one \u2192 net zero.\n\nBut over time, some turns involve drawing, some involve melding.\n\nCurrent hand: listed as 8 tiles: [W1, W3, W6, T3, B1, B2, F2, F4] \u2192 yes, 8 tiles  \nMelds: 2 chows \u2192 6 tiles  \nTotal: 14 \u2192 correct.\n\nSo I am one tile short of completion \u2014 no, in mahjong, winning hand is 14 tiles: 4\u00d73 + 2 = 14. With two melds (6 tiles), I need 8 more tiles in hand to make 14. When I win, I will have either:\n- 4 melds + 1 pair (concealed), or\n- 3 melds + 2 pairs (one being eye)\n\nBut currently I have:\n- 2 exposed chows (from Chi)\n- 8 concealed tiles\n\nTo win, I need to form two more sets (chows/pungs) and have a pair, all from those 8 tiles \u2014 very difficult.\n\nMore likely: I am far from winning. Must discard safely.\n\nRe-evaluate safety:\n\n- **T3**: Extremely dangerous. Next player performed **Chi T3** \u2014 they used a discard (possibly mine) to complete a chow involving T3. Most likely they are building sequences like T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4 or T3-T4-T5. Since they already have [T2,T3,T4], confirmed via their melds. So they have T3 in a meld. They may still need single T3 for pair or kong? Unlikely. But discarding T3 gives them opportunity to claim for kong or pair. **High risk**.\n- **F4**: North Wind. Seat wind. Only one in hand. F3 and F1 are gone. F2 has 3 copies left. F4 has 3 unseen \u2014 so not fully discarded. But no one has shown strong wind collection. Still, possible someone wants it. Medium risk.\n- **F2**: South Wind \u2014 **Prevalent Wind**. Big target. Three copies still out there. Someone may be collecting winds. Previous player discarded F1/F3/F4 \u2014 maybe not interested. But across player discarded F1/F4 \u2014 mixed. Still, **F2 is more valuable than F4** because it gives 2 points to anyone who uses it in a pung. Better to keep F2 for potential future use or discard last.\n- **B1/B2**: Dots. B5-B6-B7 melded by next player. B6/B7 all gone. B5 gone. So chows like B3-B4-B5 or B4-B5-B6 impossible. B1-B2-B3 would require B3 \u2014 four copies still available. But B4 only 2 left. B3-B4-B5 blocked. So B1-B2-B3 possible? Yes, if someone has B3. But unlikely. Also, B1/B2 are terminals. Not ideal. But **low danger** because few can use them now.\n- **W1/W3/W6**: Characters. W4-W5-W6 melded by previous player. So they had W4,W5,W6. W5 only 2 left, W6 only 1 left. So W6 is scarce. W1/W3 are safe? Possibly. But W1 is terminal, could be used in 1-2-3. W3 could be middle. But no signs of W-chows elsewhere. Likely safe.\n\nBest discard: **B1 or B2** \u2014 both are low-risk, non-honor, non-key tiles.\n\nBetween B1 and B2: B1 is terminal, B2 is simple. If I keep B1, it supports \u201cOutside Hand\u201d (if I end up with terminals/honors in every set). But I have T3 (simple), W3 (simple), etc. Not promising.\n\nAlternatively, **W6** \u2014 since W6 was just used by opponent in W4-W5-W6, and W5/W6 are nearly gone, it's unlikely they need another W6. But if they are going for kong, possible. Still, only one W6 left unshown \u2014 and one in my hand \u2192 so three already seen/discarded. That means **Last Tile** condition possible. But not relevant yet.\n\nActually, **Play B1** seems safest.\n\nBut wait: **Unseen counts**:\n- B1: 3 unshown \u2192 total 4 copies. One in my hand. So 3 elsewhere \u2192 not revealed yet. So not last tile.\n- Similarly, B2: 3 unshown \u2192 safe.\n\nAnother idea: **Could I aim for All Simples (2 points)?** Requires no terminals or honors. But I have W1, W3? W1 is terminal, W3 is simple (3), W6 simple. T3 simple. B1 terminal. F2/F4 honors. So unless I discard all terminals/honors, can't get it.\n\nNot feasible.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nSafest tile: **W1** \u2014 it's a terminal, but rarely used. No one has shown W-chows. W1 could only be used in W1-W2-W3. W2 has 4 unshown, W1 has 4 \u2014 so possible. But no evidence anyone is building it. Still, W1 is safer than T3.\n\nBut among all, **B2** stands out: number 2 dot \u2014 not terminal, not honor, not prevalent, not part of any obvious active sequence.\n\nAlso, **B1 is terminal**, so slightly more valuable to opponents (for 1-2-3). **B2 is less likely to complete a chow on its own** unless someone has B1+B3.\n\nTherefore, **B2 is safer than B1**.\n\nSimilarly, **W3** is safer than W1.\n\nBut **F4** is seat wind \u2014 should keep if possible.\n\nBest choice: **Play B2**\n\nReason: Among the valid actions, B2 is a relatively safe discard because it is a simple tile (not terminal), and given that B6-B7 are exhausted and no strong signals of dot-based chows being built beyond that, the likelihood of feeding an opponent's winning hand is minimized. T3 and F4 carry higher risk due to recent chow activity and wind value, respectively. B2 preserves flexibility while reducing exposure.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
