suits and winds.\n- Many middle tiles like W4, T6, T7, T8, B3, B5, B6, J1, J2, J3 are depleted \u2014 likely dangerous to keep.\n- The only completely untouched tile in your hand is **B8** \u2014 all four copies remain unseen, making it safest to hold or discard without aiding opponents.\n- Conversely, **F2 (South Wind)**: Only one copy remains unshown; others have been discarded or used. Since Prevalent Wind is South, someone might be collecting it. But since only one exists and you hold it, discarding it won\u2019t allow Peng (needs two). Still, it may help complete another's kong or scoring pattern.\n\nBest move should prioritize safety and minimal value to opponents.\n\nReason:  \nAmong valid actions, **B8** stands out because all four copies are still unshown \u2014 no one has used it, so it's highly unlikely any opponent is waiting on it. Discarding it now is extremely safe. Other options like W1, W3, B1, B2 could potentially complete chows for others (especially in lower numbers where many melds are forming). F2 is risky due to being Prevalent Wind \u2014 even if not pungable now, it contributes to patterns like Lesser/Greater Honors & Knitted Tiles. Thus, **discarding B8 minimizes risk** while keeping potentially useful tiles like W1/W3/B1/B2 for future chow possibilities.\n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
